<section class="section" id="hardware" aria-labelledby="hardware-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Hardware</p>
    <h2 id="hardware-title">Off-the-shelf parts in a hand-drilled box</h2>
    <p>
      Every part of Build 1 is something you can buy. The box was drilled by hand and the camera mount is
      made by hand. That shell is the part to replace first.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Part</th><th scope="col">What it is</th><th scope="col">Its job</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for row in f.hardware %}
        <tr><th scope="row">{{ row.part }}</th><td>{{ row.what }}</td><td>{{ row.job }}</td></tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="rules" aria-labelledby="rules-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">How it decides</p>
    <h2 id="rules-title">A name has to be earned</h2>
    <p>
      The first nights produced a flood of false sightings. The rule that came out of them is simple:
      a few records you can trust are worth more than many doubtful ones.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="falcon-tiers">
    <article class="falcon-tier falcon-tier--likely">
      <h3>Likely</h3>
      <p>Several frames over about two seconds agree on one species, clearly ahead of the next best name, or at least on one kind of animal. Only these appear on the Live page.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-tier falcon-tier--probable">
      <h3>Probable or possible</h3>
      <p>Look-alikes score almost the same, or the guess is faint. These wait on the Review page and are never announced.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-tier falcon-tier--confirmed">
      <h3>Confirmed</h3>
      <p>Only a person can confirm a record, after watching its recording or listening to its sound. Only confirmed visits can be posted.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <ul class="falcon-list">
    <li>The number shown beside a name is a visual similarity score. It is not a probability, and the dashboard says so.</li>
    <li>Things that stay put, such as the bowl or a stone, are learned as scenery and ignored.</li>
    <li>A strip at the edge of the picture is ignored, and one frame alone is never enough.</li>
    <li>A person's review does not retrain the models. Confirmed records are kept for good. A discarded record is hidden, and its recording is deleted a week later.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section" id="inaturalist" aria-labelledby="inaturalist-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Sharing</p>
    <h2 id="inaturalist-title">Checked visits go to iNaturalist</h2>
    <p>
      A record that stays on one computer helps nobody else. Since 16 November 2026 a visit that I have
      confirmed can be posted to <a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">iNaturalist</a>,
      where other people can check the name. Records that reach Research Grade there can be passed on to
      GBIF, the global biodiversity database.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="falcon-two">
    <div>
      <h3>What a post carries</h3>
      <ul class="falcon-list">
        <li>For a visit on camera, one to four frames from the recording, suggested by FALCON or picked by me, each with a faint FALCON mark in a corner.</li>
        <li>The date and time, the length of the visit and the weather at that moment.</li>
        <li>The bird's call, trimmed to at most a minute, when the microphones heard that species. A bird that was only heard can be posted with its call and no photograph.</li>
        <li>A note that it was recorded by an experimental station at a garden water bowl and confirmed by a person.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>What stays fixed</h3>
      <ul class="falcon-list">
        <li>Nothing is posted automatically. Every post takes my tick and my press of the Post button.</li>
        <li>Only confirmed visits can be posted, and a visit cannot be posted twice.</li>
        <li>The location is obscured. iNaturalist shows the public a random point inside a box about 20 km across, never the yard.</li>
        <li>eBird is not used. Its rules do not accept records from trail, feeder or nest cameras.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="falcon-shots falcon-shots--after">
    <figure class="falcon-shot falcon-shot--narrow">
      <a class="falcon-shot__image" href="{{ img }}/falcon-posting-968.webp?v={{ v }}">
        <span class="sr-only">Open the full-size screenshot.</span>
        <img src="{{ img }}/falcon-posting-968.webp?v={{ v }}" srcset="{{ img }}/falcon-posting-640.webp?v={{ v }} 640w, {{ img }}/falcon-posting-968.webp?v={{ v }} 968w" sizes="(max-width: 820px) 100vw, 760px" width="968" height="768" alt="FALCON posting preview for an Oriental Magpie-Robin visit: three suggested photographs, a description with the weather, and a note that only an obscured location is public." loading="lazy" decoding="async">
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        <h3>The preview before a post</h3>
        <div>
          <p>
            Ticking a confirmed visit shows exactly what would be posted: the species matched to
            iNaturalist's own list, the photographs, and a description with the weather and the length of
            the visit. The yellow line warns that this species was already posted that day. Nothing is sent
            until I press Post.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first two posts went out on 16 November:
            <a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/observations/415043198" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">an Oriental Magpie-Robin</a>,
            which reached Research Grade the same morning, and
            <a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/observations/415042739" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">a Bluethroat</a>,
            which is waiting for a second identification.
          </p>
        </div>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Results so far</p>
    <h2 id="results-title">What the sensors told us</h2>
    <p>
      An update of {{ r.asOf.date }}. The node has run since the evening of {{ r.period.from }}, with sensor
      readings in every hour, and the last two weeks went into tuning it. One yard and {{ r.period.daysWord }} days
      of one season: a first look, not a survey. A species counts only after I have checked its recording myself.
    </p>
  </div>

  <dl class="falcon-tally falcon-tally--results" aria-label="Totals from {{ r.period.from }} to {{ r.period.to }}">
    <div><dt>Species verified</dt><dd>{{ r.totals.verified }}</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Seen on camera</dt><dd>{{ r.totals.onCamera }}</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Camera visits</dt><dd>{{ r.totalsText.cameraVisits }}</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Sound records</dt><dd>{{ r.totalsText.soundRecords }}</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <div class="falcon-two falcon-species">
    <div>
      <h3>Seen on camera</h3>
      <p>{{ r.text.camera }}</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Heard only</h3>
      <p>{{ r.text.heard }}</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="falcon-note">
    {{ r.text.note }}
    {% if r.reportUrl %}The full table, with scientific names, methods and limits, is in the
    <a href="{{ r.reportUrl }}">field report</a>.{% endif %}
  </p>

  <h3 class="falcon-subhead">Surprises and regulars</h3>
  <div class="falcon-results">
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--person">A surprise</span>
      <h3>White-breasted Waterhen</h3>
      <p>{{ r.notable.waterhen.times }}, on {{ r.notable.waterhen.date }} at {{ r.notable.waterhen.time }}. A bird of wet ground, at a bowl in a city backyard.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--person">A surprise</span>
      <h3>Rhesus Macaque</h3>
      <p>On {{ r.notable.macaque.date }} at {{ r.notable.macaque.time }} a macaque came down to the bowl, the first monkey on FALCON's record.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--person">Shy, and a regular</span>
      <h3>Bluethroat</h3>
      <p>
        A shy winter visitor that keeps coming back: {{ r.notable.bluethroat.visits }} visits on
        {{ r.notable.bluethroat.cameraDays }} of the {{ r.notable.bluethroat.daysSinceFirst }} days since
        {{ r.notable.bluethroat.date }}, most of them between 15:00 and 17:00.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--person">Back again</span>
      <h3>Sulphur-bellied Warbler</h3>
      <p>On {{ r.notable.warbler.date }} at {{ r.notable.warbler.time }}. I photographed one in this yard last year too.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  {% if r.text.otherNew %}
  <p class="falcon-note falcon-note--after">Also new at the bowl since the last update: {{ r.text.otherNew }}.</p>
  {% endif %}

  <h3 class="falcon-subhead">When the bowl is busy</h3>
  <p class="falcon-note">
    Each square is one hour of one day. The camera is busiest from 08:00 to 10:00 and again from 14:00 to
    17:00. The calls start at dawn: most come between 06:00 and 10:00 and in the hour from 17:00. The
    Rose-ringed Parakeets, drawn by a fig tree in the yard, are the loudest at both ends of the day.
  </p>
  <div class="falcon-two falcon-heats">
    {% set heats = [
      { key: "camera", title: "Camera visits", unit: "visits", tone: "camera" },
      { key: "sound", title: "Sound records", unit: "records", tone: "sound" }
    ] %}
    {% for h in heats %}
    <figure class="falcon-heat falcon-heat--{{ h.tone }}">
      <figcaption>{{ h.title }}, by day and hour</figcaption>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col"><span class="sr-only">Day</span></th>{% for hr in r.hours %}<th scope="col">{% if hr % 6 == 0 %}{{ hr }}{% else %}<span class="sr-only">{{ hr }}</span>{% endif %}</th>{% endfor %}</tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {% for row in r[h.key] %}
            <tr><th scope="row">{{ row.label }}</th>{% for c in row.cells %}<td class="l{{ c.l }}" title="{{ row.label }}, {{ loop.index0 }}:00, {{ c.n }} {{ h.unit }}"><span class="sr-only">{{ c.n }}</span></td>{% endfor %}</tr>
            {% endfor %}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </figure>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
  <p class="falcon-heat-key" aria-hidden="true">
    <span><i class="l0"></i>none</span><span><i class="l1"></i>1–2</span><span><i class="l2"></i>3–5</span><span><i class="l3"></i>6–11</span><span><i class="l4"></i>12 or more</span>
  </p>

  <figure class="falcon-heat falcon-heat--species">
    <figcaption>The eight commonest species, seen and heard, by hour of the day</figcaption>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col"><span class="sr-only">Species</span></th>{% for hr in r.hours %}<th scope="col">{% if hr % 3 == 0 %}{{ hr }}{% else %}<span class="sr-only">{{ hr }}</span>{% endif %}</th>{% endfor %}</tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for row in r.species %}
          <tr><th scope="row">{{ row.label }} <small>{{ row.total }}, {{ row.how }}</small></th>{% for c in row.cells %}<td class="l{{ c.l }}" title="{{ row.label }}, {{ loop.index0 }}:00, {{ c.n }} records"><span class="sr-only">{{ c.n }}</span></td>{% endfor %}</tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="falcon-note">Shaded against each species' own busiest hour. Rose-ringed Parakeets are heard nearly all day but never come to the bowl; the Bluethroat comes late in the afternoon.</p>
  </figure>

  <div class="falcon-reading">
    <p>
      <b>Weather.</b> The node logs temperature, humidity and rain all the time. It measured
      {{ r.weatherRange.min }} to {{ r.weatherRange.max }} °C, and the rain plate read wet on
      {{ r.weatherRange.wetDaysWord }} days only. The bowl is quietest in the warmest hours, but those are also
      the middle of the day, so {{ r.period.daysWord }} days of one season cannot tell heat apart from the hour.
      No weather pattern can be claimed yet.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="falcon-subhead">What FALCON does by itself, and what I do</h3>
  <ul class="falcon-list">
    <li><b>Capturing is automatic.</b> FALCON recorded every one of the {{ r.rates.camera.realText }} verified camera visits and {{ r.rates.sound.realText }} verified sound records by itself, each with its recording and the weather at that moment.</li>
    <li><b>By sound, the names hold.</b> When BirdNET heard a real bird, its name was right for {{ r.rates.sound.rightText }} of the {{ r.rates.sound.realText }} records ({{ r.rates.sound.percent }} %). Street and household noise is still sometimes named as a bird, so every record gets a check.</li>
    <li><b>From the picture, the names are the weak point.</b> With this webcam the suggested name was often wrong, and I named most visits myself from the recordings. The picture is poor in low light, pink in the mornings and off in colour: a Bluethroat's throat did not look blue. I put this down to the camera, not to the AI models.</li>
    <li><b>What is being done.</b> This is why the first support money went into a better camera and microphone for Build 2.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="falcon-subhead">Two weeks of tuning</h3>
  <div class="falcon-results falcon-tuning">
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--measured">Where it started</span>
      <h3>The first nights</h3>
      <ul class="falcon-list">
        <li>On the first night 133 “visits” were house geckos under wrong names, and a leaf or stone at the edge of the picture was named a civet.</li>
        <li>The camera's species model chose from 14,367 species of all India.</li>
        <li>Evidence was a silent 10-second clip, and one bathing bird became six visits.</li>
        <li>A bad camera restart could break the picture into blocks.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--measured">Calibrated</span>
      <h3>Picture, sound and lists</h3>
      <ul class="falcon-list">
        <li>The camera's picture controls were tuned by hand and saved, and the detector's settings were chosen by a test on saved clips.</li>
        <li>The microphone was measured: bird calls reach only about 13 dB above its hiss.</li>
        <li>The camera now chooses from 1,384 species recorded in Rajasthan.</li>
        <li>BirdNET names only birds recorded in Rajasthan. Any other call is kept as an unidentified sound to check later.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--person">Improved</span>
      <h3>Recording and review</h3>
      <ul class="falcon-list">
        <li>Each stay at the water is one recording with its sound, and one card to review.</li>
        <li>A sound is announced only when the raw and a cleaned copy of the recording agree on the bird.</li>
        <li>Damaged frames are thrown away and the camera restarts itself.</li>
        <li>The Live and Review pages were rebuilt for the phone, and checked visits can be posted to iNaturalist.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--unsure">What spoiled the data</span>
      <h3>Pink mornings and rats</h3>
      <ul class="falcon-list">
        <li><b>Pink footage.</b> The webcam leaves its night setting late. On the three mornings I measured, the daylight picture stayed pink until 09:30 or later, in the busiest hours. Colours were lost, a Bluethroat's throat did not look blue, and names went wrong.</li>
        <li><b>Rats.</b> I put grain near the bowl. Only House Sparrows and Laughing Doves fed on it, and from 19 November rats raided it every night, at least {% for n in r.rats %}{{ n.visits }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} visits over five nights. Their records buried everything else and were deleted, and rats at night are no longer logged. No rat snake or owl followed, so there will be no more feed near the bowl.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h3 class="falcon-subhead">Known limits</h3>
  <ul class="falcon-list">
    <li>Night vision reaches about a metre, and insects in it cannot be named.</li>
    <li>A name counts only after a person has checked it, and reviewing is the slowest part.</li>
    <li>It needs a PC with an NVIDIA graphics card indoors.</li>
    <li>The box is drilled by hand and has not been through a monsoon or a summer.</li>
    <li>It logs visits to one spot. It does not count animals or estimate populations.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section" id="roadmap" aria-labelledby="roadmap-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Where it is going</p>
    <h2 id="roadmap-title">From a backyard rig to something others can host</h2>
    <p>
      The aim is a device that a friend, a school or a field researcher can plug in and use without a
      desktop PC or any technical setup. Hardware is bought one piece at a time, and each step has to pass
      a test before the next one starts.
    </p>
  </div>
  <ol class="falcon-roadmap">
    {% for step in f.roadmap %}
    {% set stateClass = "falcon-state--" + (step.state | lower | replace(" ", "-")) %}
    <li class="{{ stateClass }}">
      <span class="falcon-state {{ stateClass }}">{{ step.state }}</span>
      <h3>{{ step.name }}</h3>
      <p>{{ step.text }}</p>
    </li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="section falcon-support" id="support" aria-labelledby="support-title">
  <div class="falcon-support__panel">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Support the next build</p>
      <h2 id="support-title">Two pieces of equipment would move this forward</h2>
      <p>
        Build 1 leans on a desktop PC and a shell made by hand. Two purchases would turn
        it into something other people can copy, and I am asking readers to help with them. Prices are what
        Indian sellers listed on {{ f.pricesChecked }}.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="falcon-asks">
      <article class="falcon-ask">
        <h3>A small computer for the AI</h3>
        <p class="falcon-ask__price">about ₹65,000 to ₹70,000</p>
        <dl>
          <div>
            <dt>What</dt>
            <dd>An NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano Super developer kit with a solid-state drive, a fan case and a Wi-Fi card. The kit alone was listed between ₹52,000 and ₹69,300 at three sellers.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Why</dt>
            <dd>Today every model runs on a desktop PC with a gaming graphics card. A home, a school or a field station without one cannot host a node.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>How it will be tested</dt>
            <dd>FALCON has not run on a Jetson yet. The models are converted for it, and it then has to give the same answers as the PC on nights already recorded. The result goes on this page either way.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </article>
      <article class="falcon-ask">
        <h3>A simple 3D printer</h3>
        <p class="falcon-ask__price">about ₹25,000 to ₹30,000</p>
        <dl>
          <div>
            <dt>What</dt>
            <dd>A small desktop printer such as the Bambu Lab A1 mini, with filament for outdoor parts. The printer alone was listed between ₹17,300 and ₹21,500.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Why</dt>
            <dd>A hood that lets the light sensor see daylight, a shaded holder for the weather sensor, a cradle for the rain plate and a post mount. Printed parts can be made the same way twice and shared as files.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>How it will be tested</dt>
            <dd>The first prints are the hood and the holder for the weather sensor, tried on the node in the yard. The repository already holds an enclosure drawn for an earlier camera, not printed yet. Which plastic stands a Kota summer is part of the test.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="falcon-total">
      <p><strong>{{ f.fund.goal }}</strong>for both, with the smaller parts they need.</p>
      <a class="button-link" href="{{ f.supportUrl }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Contribute on Buy Me a Coffee</a>
    </div>

    <dl class="falcon-tally" aria-label="Running total">
      <div><dt>Received for FALCON</dt><dd>{{ f.fund.received }}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Spent</dt><dd>{{ f.fund.spent }}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Bought so far</dt><dd>{{ f.fund.bought }}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Updated</dt><dd>{{ f.fund.updated }}</dd></div>
    </dl>

    {% if f.fund.supporters and f.fund.supporters.length %}
    <div class="falcon-thanks">
      <h3 id="thanks-title">Thank you</h3>
      <p class="falcon-note">The people who have supported FALCON so far.</p>
      <ul class="falcon-mugs" aria-labelledby="thanks-title">
        {# A first name of twelve letters or more is fitted to the width of the mug. #}
        {% for s in f.fund.supporters %}{% set n = s.first | length %}
        <li class="falcon-mug">
          <span class="falcon-mug__name">{{ s.name }}</span>
          <svg class="falcon-mug__cup" viewBox="0 0 158 96" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path class="falcon-mug__steam" fill="none" d="M45 20c-6-5 6-9 0-15M68 20c-6-5 6-9 0-15M91 20c-6-5 6-9 0-15"/>
            <path class="falcon-mug__handle" fill="none" d="M124 42h7a13 13 0 0 1 0 26h-7"/>
            <path class="falcon-mug__body" d="M12 28h112v42a16 16 0 0 1-16 16H28a16 16 0 0 1-16-16z"/>
            <text class="falcon-mug__first{% if n > 14 %} falcon-mug__first--small{% endif %}" x="68" y="57" dy="0.35em" text-anchor="middle"{% if n > 11 %} textLength="98" lengthAdjust="spacingAndGlyphs"{% endif %}>{{ s.first }}</text>
          </svg>
        </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </div>
    {% endif %}

    <div class="falcon-terms">
      <div>
        <h3>How the money is handled</h3>
        <ul class="falcon-list">
          <li>Contributions go through the Cane &amp; Camera page on Buy Me a Coffee, the same page that supports the upkeep of MHTR.in. <b>Please write FALCON in your message</b> so the money is counted here. Without that word it goes to the upkeep of the site, as before.</li>
          <li><b>A change of plan.</b> The first money went into Build 2's camera and microphone instead of the printer, because the results above showed that the webcam and the old microphone held FALCON back most. From here on, money marked FALCON goes to the two items above, the printer first.</li>
          <li>The running total above is updated by hand whenever money comes in or something is bought.</li>
          <li>If less comes in than the computer costs, that money stays set aside for it, and this page will say how much is there.</li>
          <li>This is a personal project, not a registered charity. A contribution is a gift: there are no rewards, no shares, no device in return and no tax benefit.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Other ways to help</h3>
        <ul class="falcon-list">
          <li><b>Lend hardware.</b> A Jetson you no longer use, or a few hours on your own 3D printer, does the same job as money.</li>
          <li><b>Lend your ears.</b> If you know the night calls of owls around Kota and can check a few short recordings, write to me.</li>
          <li><b>Try the code.</b> Follow the install guide on your own computer and report where it breaks.</li>
          <li><b>Identify on iNaturalist.</b> If you know the birds of Rajasthan, the <a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/observations/415042739" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bluethroat of 16 November</a> is waiting for a second identification.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="falcon-note">Write to <a href="mailto:{{ site.contactEmail }}">{{ site.contactEmail }}</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="code" aria-labelledby="code-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Open source</p>
    <h2 id="code-title">Build one, or help build the next</h2>
    <p>
      Everything written for FALCON is in one public repository: the node service for the Raspberry Pi, the
      application that runs the models, the dashboard, the tests and the build notes. Build 1 is tagged v1.0.
      The work done since then, which is the whole-visit recordings, the simpler review page and the posting
      to iNaturalist, runs at home and follows as the next release when its testing is finished.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="falcon-two">
    <div>
      <ul class="falcon-list">
        <li>The FALCON code is free software under the GNU AGPL, version 3. You can use it, change it and share it. If you pass on a changed version, or run one for other people, you share your changes under the same licence, keep the author credit and give your version its own name.</li>
        <li>Build 1 (v1.0) was published under the Apache 2.0 licence and stays available under it.</li>
        <li>The models are not part of the repository. FALCON downloads them, and each keeps its own licence.</li>
        <li>The BirdNET model is for non-commercial use only. Anyone thinking of selling a device has to replace it or license it.</li>
        <li>Changes come in as pull requests.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button-link" href="{{ f.repoUrl }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FALCON on GitHub</a>
        <a class="button-link button-link-secondary" href="{{ f.repoUrl }}/tree/v1.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Build 1 (v1.0)</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <dl class="falcon-licences" aria-label="Licences">
      <div><dt>FALCON code</dt><dd>GNU AGPL version 3, with <a href="{{ f.repoUrl }}/blob/main/ADDITIONAL-TERMS.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">terms on author credit and naming</a>. Build 1 (v1.0): Apache 2.0.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>MegaDetector V6</dt><dd>MIT variant, through PyTorchWildlife. Finds animals, people and vehicles.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>BioCLIP 2.5</dt><dd>MIT. Suggests the species.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>BirdNET V2.4</dt><dd>Code MIT; model CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, which means non-commercial use only.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>MediaMTX</dt><dd>MIT. Serves the video and sound from the node.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Species list</dt><dd>Built from research-grade records on iNaturalist.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="care" aria-labelledby="care-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Care and privacy</p>
    <h2 id="care-title">It watches wildlife, not people</h2>
  </div>
  <ul class="falcon-list">
    <li>People are never saved as observations. By default a person in the picture is covered with a grey box, live and in saved recordings, and seconds with human voices are silenced in saved sound, including sound attached to a post on iNaturalist. These filters are automatic and can miss, so anyone who hosts a node should still tell the people who live there and the neighbours within earshot.</li>
    <li>The models run on the owner's computer. No picture or sound is sent to a cloud service for recognition. A record leaves that computer only when the owner exports it or posts it to iNaturalist.</li>
    <li>This node stands in a private backyard in Kota city. Using a device like it inside a protected area needs written permission from the Forest Department.</li>
    <li>This page gives the place only as Kota, Rajasthan. Exact locations of wildlife records are not published here, and the posts on iNaturalist carry an obscured location.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

